Test a plan under geological, processing, operating, and external uncertainty (@mine-planning-uncertainty: 0, is #chapter, #part-of @mining-and-mineral-engineering, #source "../sources/Operations-Research-in-Mine-Planning.lingua#r_3WX1KW34EWEGYZ6HGEMGGQBKHS", #source "../sources/MineLib-Benchmarks-and-Methods.lingua#r_3KXVJNVZ2FSQ8CZJMJG2JDQYR8", #source "../sources/Geostatistical-Declustering-and-Variograms.lingua#r_1MDWJYNM88E34YCM4918C7DEEQ", #source "../../../inquiry/reproducible-computation/Reproducible-computation.lingua#r_78FWWJDCV0ZGG7VYSPRCWN56DQ", #mining-and-mineral-engineering "From-long-range-plan-to-observed-operating-work.lingua#r_28TW1F4Z2GG8HTSTF4N3D8SPXC") { r_0MGGJWBA361D5QMMW1SQF6GPN4
**Read:** [A Review of Operations Research in Mine Planning — Newman and colleagues](../sources/Operations-Research-in-Mine-Planning.lingua#r_3WX1KW34EWEGYZ6HGEMGGQBKHS), the full relevant uncertainty/planning primary methods reached through the review; [MineLib — Espinoza, Goycoolea, Moreno, and Newman](../sources/MineLib-Benchmarks-and-Methods.lingua#r_3KXVJNVZ2FSQ8CZJMJG2JDQYR8), the exact complete base-model/data definition; [Geostatistical sampling, continuity, estimation, and simulation — Deutsch and contributors](../sources/Geostatistical-Declustering-and-Variograms.lingua#r_1MDWJYNM88E34YCM4918C7DEEQ), the full selected uncertainty/domain methods; [Reproducible computational work](../../../inquiry/reproducible-computation/Reproducible-computation.lingua#r_78FWWJDCV0ZGG7VYSPRCWN56DQ), the complete stochastic, numerical, and reproducibility studies used.

A plan is conditional on geological geometry/grade, processing response, ground/water, equipment performance, costs, prices, people, and other conditions. Separate uncertain inputs, model structure, decisions, and observations available over time. A deterministic optimum can be brittle to plausible changes in those assumptions.

Scenario comparisons need coherent joint conditions and timing. Independent random perturbations can miss correlations or produce physically inconsistent states. More simulated cases do not compensate for omitted geological alternatives, biased sampling, or a wrong process model.

Distinguish expected performance, dispersion/tail behavior, feasibility under scenarios, regret, and the value of additional information. A strategy allowing future adaptation differs from a fixed schedule evaluated after uncertainty is revealed. Avoid giving a model knowledge that would not be available when the decision is made.

**Work:** extend a documented planning problem with explicit scenarios or alternative geological/process models. Reproduce the base result, independently verify feasibility/bounds, and compare a fixed and a stated adaptive policy. Identify which observation could change the plan and whether its timing/cost/support actually addresses the controlling uncertainty.

Read complete primary methods from the operations-research review and the selected benchmark specification. The advanced project requires explaining what the analysis establishes about the model, what remains operationally uncertain, and which new evidence would improve the decision.

**Continue:** [Translate a strategic plan into tasks, readiness, and feedback at shorter timescales](From-long-range-plan-to-observed-operating-work.lingua#r_28TW1F4Z2GG8HTSTF4N3D8SPXC).

**Return:** [Mining and mineral engineering — extraction, processing, and continuing work](../Mining-and-mineral-engineering.lingua#r_22HJPS3RTK5EX1J55YJP75PHYA).
